WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
In this video, you use SUSE AI Factory in Rancher to deploy an AI stack on the GPU cluster: the NVIDIA GPU Operator, vLLM serving a model, and Open WebUI.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:19.990
In Rancher, SUSE AI Factory deploys AI stacks from blueprints. Each blueprint bundles Helm charts with tested values.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:23.570
Show all catalogs, including custom blueprints,

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.810
and search for the blueprint prepared for this lab.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:34.680
It bundles the NVIDIA GPU Operator, vLLM from the SUSE Application Collection serving Qwen 2.5 0.5B Instruct, and Open WebUI.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.990
Select Install.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:41.260
Keep the instance name and deploy into the suse-ai namespace.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.860
Fleet deploys the bundle to the clusters you select. Clear the cluster the wizard preselects,

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.890
and select demo-ai, the GPU cluster on SUSE Virtualization.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:02.010
You can override each component's Helm values here. Keep the blueprint defaults.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:13.460
Review the plan. The GPU Operator goes to its own namespace, vLLM and Open WebUI to suse-ai, all on demo-ai.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.370
Select Install.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:25.460
Open Workloads. The new AI workload is listed, and Fleet rolls out the three components to demo-ai.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:34.820
The GPU Operator set up the NVIDIA driver on the GPU worker, and vLLM downloaded the model and loaded it onto the T400. The workload is Running.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:40.470
Select Open in Rancher to open the suse-ai namespace on demo-ai.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.260
Open its Workloads tab.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:54.530
The vLLM engine for Qwen 2.5 0.5B Instruct is Active, with the image from the SUSE Application Collection, next to the router and Open WebUI.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:02:04.525
SUSE AI runs on the guest cluster and vLLM serves the model on the passed-through GPU. Next, you query it and watch its metrics.
